- op: discuss  # 39eb
  at: '2026-09-20T10:04:35Z'  # 39eb
  by: agent  # 39eb
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 39eb
  clock: 3  # 39eb
  nonce: 39eb  # 39eb
  message: "SATISFIED, but the row's own root-cause sentence was wrong and that is worth recording.\n\nWHAT I FILED. \"The SCIP translation layer stamps origin=\"scip\" — the backend field — while the analyzers register under the names rust_analyzer and scip_python, and nothing asserts the two agree.\"\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE. `scip` is not the backend field leaking. It is a DECLARED synthetic pass id: `catalog._SYNTHETIC_PASS_IDS` lists it beside `orchestrator_file_symbol_synthesis` and `boundary_external_symbol_synthesis`, and `all_known_pass_ids()` resolves it, per ADR-0044 (\"these are genuine synthetic pass IDs, not a separate synthesis-mechanism axis\"). Verified: `'scip' in all_known_pass_ids()` is True. So `Edge.origin == \"scip\"` satisfies its `# axis: pass-id` annotation, and the shared SCIP translation (`scip/index.py`, `scip/edges.py`, `scip/calls.py`) genuinely is one pass serving two arms. That it spells the same as `RegisteredAnalyzer.backend` is a coincidence of naming.\n\nTHE REAL DEFECT, which is narrower and worse. `anchored_producer_tokens` — the ADR-0057 §14 origin condition I landed in PR #1100 — bridged the analyzer-name / origin-token gap by unioning `RegisteredAnalyzer.backend`. That is reading the right answer off the wrong field. The registry's own docstring says `backend` is \"Decoupled from pass_id so the ID stays stable across backend swaps\", so:\n  - a backend swap, or a producer whose backend label and origin token differ, disarms §14 for that producer with NO error (false-negative direction: a stub that should be demoted is not);\n  - it silently admitted `ast` — the incumbent Python analyzer's parsing backend — as a Python producer token.\n\nTHE FIX. `RegisteredAnalyzer.emits_origin` declares the origin token a producer's records carry; `None` means \"the registration name\", which is true for every analyzer except these two, so the declaration marks the exception instead of adding noise to a hundred rows. `anchored_producer_tokens` reads the declaration. Absent is not empty here either: `None` is a real default, not a missing answer.\n\nEVIDENCE (the statement, checked):\n  - `anchored_producer_tokens(\"python\")` == {\"python\", \"scip_python\", \"scip\"} — `ast` gone\n  - `anchored_producer_tokens(\"rust\")`   == {\"rust\", \"rust_analyzer\", \"scip\"}\n  - every declared `emits_origin` is in `all_known_pass_ids()`, so a declaration cannot invent a vocabulary\n  - exactly two analyzers declare one; `rust` / `javascript` / `svelte` / `vue` declare `None`\n  - PER ARM, IN ITS OWN PACKAGE, the declaration is checked against the EMISSION: every symbol and edge the recorded translation produces carries the declared token, with a non-empty guard so it cannot pass vacuously. On the scip-python sample project that is 11 symbols + 17 edges; on aardvark-dns the recorded Rust index.\n\nWHAT IS NOT ASSERTED. I did not enumerate every producer's emitted origin against its registration — only the two that declare an exception are checked against their emissions, plus the four sentinels that declare none. A producer that stamps a token differing from its registration name and declares nothing would still pass; making THAT impossible needs a corpus-wide emission check, which the recorded fixtures cannot supply for analyzers with no recorded fixture."  # 39eb
